# Tactic Board — Squad Fit Finder

## FM27 Plus app (reads the game directly — no export)

`dist\FM27Plus.exe` is this same tool wrapped in a small Windows app that reads
your **open Football Manager 27 save straight out of the game's memory**, so
there is no CSV export step. Start FM27, load your save, then double-click
`FM27Plus.exe`. (FM26 is still supported since the layout is identical, but the
latest changes were only tested on FM27; if both games are running it reads the
newest one that has a save loaded. The older `FM26Plus.exe` is the FM26-only
build and can be deleted.) It scans the whole database (~130k players) in about 3 seconds
and loads your squad into every tab (Squad Depth, Tactical Fit, Tactic Advisor,
Team Info, Youngsters, Potential, Player Search, Financial) automatically. The *Scouting Pool* panel pulls a
filtered shortlist from the **entire database** (age, potential, position,
nation...) and ranks it against your tactic; *Scan Game* refreshes everything
(press it again after loading a different save or advancing the game).

**Scouting Pool filters:** age, potential (PA), current ability (CA), position,
nation, **min / max transfer value (£M)**, and **Exclude players who need a work
permit**. The permit check is by nationality, for a club in *your club's*
country: his own nationality or any of his secondary nationalities (the ones
the game lists on the Personal tab) must give free movement - UK + Ireland for
an English club, EU/EEA/Switzerland for a club in those areas, otherwise only
the club's own country. The game's real approval uses a points system and can
differ, so treat it as a guide; rows that fail the check are tagged "permit".

**Player interest in my club** works like the game's own *Player Interest* filter: pick
*Transfer or loan*, *Transfer only* or *Loan only* and then *Interested*, *Persuadable* or *Doubtful*
(each level includes the keener ones), or *Not considered* for players who wouldn't come at all; *Any*
switches it off. Every player gets two levels, one for a permanent **transfer** and one for a **loan**,
shown as **T** / **L** tags in the Scouting tab's *Interest* column. The game's rule is private, so this
is an **estimate**, not a value read from the game:
- nobody whose world reputation is more than ~100 above your club's is ever interested (the game's lists
  stop at exactly that ceiling - the one part measured against the game);
- below that, the base level is how big his club is compared with yours (club "stature" = the average
  reputation of a club's 18 best players, since the game stores a club reputation for only about half the
  clubs), and it moves one level up or down for: whether he'd walk into your XI while not being a regular
  at his club, being one of his club's five best players, ambition, loyalty, a contract with six months or
  less left, and your club being in his own country;
- loans use looser thresholds (a season at a smaller club for games is an easy sell), go up for players
  outside their club's best 11 who'd be in your squad, down for key players, players who wouldn't make your
  best 22 and players over 28, and are impossible for free agents and players already out on loan; free
  agents are always interested in a transfer.

Every threshold is a constant at the top of `fm26mem/interest.py`. They have **not** been checked against
the game's own Player Interest lists yet - see `CALIBRATION.md`.

**Home-grown status** (the *If Home-Grown Status Is / Is not ...* row) is **read from the game**, not
worked out: it is the list the game shows on a player's *Personal → Eligibility* screen (*Nation
Eligibility* / *Club Eligibility*), which it keeps with each player. *Trained in nation (15-21)* = he is
listed "Homegrown 15-21" and *Fully Eligible* for that nation, *Trained at club (15-21)* = the same for your
club, plus the *(0-21)* versions, which also accept the 15-21 rows. As on that screen, a club row also
counts for the club's country (a youth-intake player listed "KäPa 0-21" is Finland 0-21), and rows still in
progress ("243/1095 days") don't count yet. Checked against the game's Eligibility screen for eight
players, and against the HGN tags in the KäPa squad. Country-specific types (Belgium's "0-23 or Belgian",
England's "0-19 (1 year)", ...) are not offered.

**Max players:** leave it blank (the default) and the pool loads **every player
that matches your filters** - about 12,500 with the default age ≤ 24 / potential
≥ 120 - best potential first. Type a number to load only that many. Either way
the pool never holds more than 40,000 players (`POOL_CEILING` in `server.py`);
if more match, the status line says "40,000 of N matching players loaded" and the
fix is to tighten a filter. The Scouting tab ranks the pool against your tactic
only while you are looking at it (about 1 s for 12,500 players, about 3 s for
40,000), so editing your tactic on the other tabs stays instant. Each position's
table starts with the best 200 and has **Show 200 more / Show 1,000 more**
buttons; the filters at the top of the tab (search, max age, min fit, min
potential) apply to the whole ranked pool at once.

**Hide injured players:** on the Squad Depth, Tactical Fit and Tactic Advisor tabs a
"Hide injured players (n injured)" box leaves injured players out of the depth chart,
the suggested XI and the position tables (the next best player steps up); untick it
and they all come back. With it unticked, injured players are marked with a red
"Injured" tag (hover it for the injury). Injuries are read from two places in the
game's memory and merged - the Medical Centre's "Current Injuries" list (injuries
older than about a week) plus the newer injury records (the last week or so; the game
keeps those elsewhere). The Medical Centre list is found from the entries that carry its
usual marker values and is then **read in full**, so an injury whose entry looks different
(a filled-in date, say) is no longer missed as long as it sits in the same list - the old
reader only counted entries with the exact marker. The original version was checked against
the Medical Centre (6 of 6 and 4 of 4); the full-list read has only been tested on synthetic
data so far. A scan takes about 3 seconds longer because of it, and it refreshes
whenever you press *Scan Game*. Only your **first team** is covered: injured U21 / U18
players, and other clubs' players, are not detected (they read as fit).
`http://127.0.0.1:47027/api/debug/injuries` shows exactly what was found (every list entry,
every recent injury record and why it was or wasn't counted), to compare with the Medical Centre.

**Condition and match sharpness:** every squad table (Best XI, Squad Depth, Tactical Fit,
bench) has a "Con / Shp" column with each player's condition and match sharpness today, in
per cent, read from the game (FM27 itself only shows them as icons). They are read again
every time the squad loads, so they stay current without a full rescan. Hover a value for
what it costs. **Use today's fitness** (toolbar, Squad Depth / Tactical Fit / Tactic Advisor /
Team Info) takes that cost off each rating, so the Best XI and the Tactic Advisor pick for
today's match. Fm-Match-Lab's Study 5 replayed real FM27 matches with a whole team at a set
level. Against fully fit (the confirmation rerun: full condition +0.13, full sharpness +0.10),
75% sharpness cost 0.47 points per game and 50% cost 1.13; 90% condition cost 0.41 and 80% cost
0.93. That is more than any attribute or tactic setting. Shared over the eleven starters, one
player at 75% sharpness costs about 0.04 points per game. That is worth about 1.3 points off each
of his attributes, since one point on every attribute the engine rewards is worth about 0.032 per
starter (Study 1). Team Info's **Fitness today** lists the XI's tired or rusty starters with what
each costs, and the players outside the XI who need minutes to get sharp. The values were checked against
FM's own icons and condition bands for the 75 players of a live save (see `fm26mem/layout.py`).

**Position order:** every page that lists positions (Squad Depth, Best XI,
Tactical Fit, Potential, Scouting, the Position Fit selector) shows them
goalkeeper first, then back to front and left to right - defenders, wing-backs
and defensive mids, midfielders, attacking mids, strikers - whatever order you
clicked them in. (Who is *picked* for each slot still follows the order the
slots were added, so the suggestions themselves don't change.)

**It keeps working whatever the game is doing.** A scan can catch the game in the
middle of something, so it retries by itself, and a failed rescan never throws
away your last good data. Retired players (the game wipes their player ID),
unused slots and any stale duplicate copy of a player are ignored - the live
copy is picked by checking which one its contract points back at. If you have
managed more than one club in a save, a *Squad club* selector appears. The
window connects on its own when the game starts or restarts.

What you get that an export doesn't have: real **Current/Potential Ability
(0-200)**, all **hidden attributes** (Consistency, Important Matches,
Versatility, Injury Proneness, Ambition, Professionalism, ...), exact transfer
value, wage and contract expiry, and every player in the game — not just a
visible list. The Potential tab uses the real PA as the ceiling.

Notes:
- It is strictly **read-only**: it opens the game process with read access only
  and never writes to it or to your save. Use it for single-player saves.
- Nothing leaves your machine. The app serves the UI on `127.0.0.1:47027`
  and opens it in an Edge/Chrome app window; closing the window quits it.
- "Your squad" = whoever the database says is at your club *right now*: each
  player's current club is his loan club if he is on loan, otherwise the club
  that holds his contract (no contract = free agent). So players you sold,
  released or never signed (e.g. turned-down youth-intake prospects) drop out
  as soon as you rescan. Players on loan *to* you stay in (they can play, but
  are never offered as sellable); your own players loaned out are left out
  unless you tick *Include my players on loan elsewhere*. The game's old
  "human club" flag is deliberately not used for this: it never clears when a
  player leaves. Players with no club at all (free agents and the engine's
  pre-generated youth prospects) are hidden from the pool unless you tick
  *Include unattached players*.
- **Closing the window closes the app** - and nothing else does. The page says goodbye as it
  closes; a reload or a second window carries on. Earlier versions quit when the window hadn't
  checked in for 25 seconds, but Edge / Chrome slow a minimised or covered window (a full-screen
  Football Manager) to about one check-in a minute, so the app quit while you were playing and
  every request from the window then failed ("Failed to fetch") until it was reopened. Now a quiet
  window only ends the app if it went quiet while it was visible (a crashed browser), after 8 hours
  of silence, or 75 seconds after its goodbye. If the app does stop, the window says so plainly.
- If Windows SmartScreen / Defender warns about the unsigned exe, that's
  expected for any program that reads another process's memory.
- If FM runs as administrator, run `FM27Plus.exe` as administrator too.
- Log file for troubleshooting: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\FM27Plus\log.txt`.
- Diagnostics: `/api/debug/injuries` (above) and `/api/debug/squad` (players the game still
  marks as yours who aren't counted in the squad, with the club each one is at now).
- Opening `index.html` directly still works as before with CSV imports.

**FM27 vs FM26:** the player record layout is identical (checked against the
game's own player screens: every attribute, feet, height, value, wage, expiry,
age, position, squad sizes). Differences that are handled: the game date
is the newest "last updated" stamp (in a fresh save only your own squad has
been stamped), ~600 unused page slots (tag `0xFFFFFFFF`) are skipped, and a
retired player keeps his record but his player ID becomes `0xFFFFFFFF`.

**After a game update** the in-memory layout can move. `fm26mem/layout.py`
holds every offset (record size, attribute block, CA/PA, name pointers, ...)
and the scanner validates them on every scan; if something no longer fits it
says "layout not recognised" instead of showing wrong data. The scan finds the
player pages structurally (the same engine vtable repeating every 968 bytes),
so only the offsets in `layout.py` ever need re-calibrating.

To rebuild the exe: double-click `build_exe.bat` (or `.\build_exe.bat` in PowerShell). It needs Python 3 on
PATH and builds in its own environment, `.build-venv`, which it sets up from `requirements.txt` on the first run
(needs internet). Packages in your main Python can't interfere with it. To run from source:
`pip install -r requirements.txt`, then `python server.py`.

---

## What the app shows (tabs)

| Tab | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Squad Depth** | Top three players for every slot of your tactic, nobody used twice |
| **Tactical Fit** | The best XI (an optimal assignment, not a greedy pick), plus the **bench** (reserve keeper first, then the player who covers the most ground) and the **rotation XI** (each slot's second choice), then a ranked table per slot |
| **Tactic Advisor** | Scores your pitch tactic, every saved formation and eight presets (4-2-3-1, 4-3-3, 4-4-2, 4-1-4-1, 4-3-2-1, 3-5-2, 3-4-3, 5-3-2) by the best XI your squad can field in them. Presets use the roles that suit your squad best; **Use on pitch** loads one |
| **Team Info** | Squad at a glance: what the match engine rewards (fitness today and who needs minutes, out-of-position starters, the XI on the top attributes), the stars, brightest prospects, who to loan out for minutes, who needs a new contract, surplus players, injuries |
| **Youngsters** | Everyone 19 / 21 / 23 or under with CA, PA, best roles, current vs potential rating, a growth score and a verdict (*Wonderkid*, *Future star*, *Future starter*, *Squad player*, *Ready now*), and a **future XI** built from them by potential |
| **Potential** | How good each squad player could become in each slot (PA ceiling, age, growth attributes) |
| **Player Search** | One player: attributes, his **match-engine** breakdown (what each attribute the engine rewards is worth for him), a **hidden profile** (personality, hidden attributes, the game's tactical ability, availability), and his best roles in every position |
| **Financial** | Surplus players by transfer value |
| **Scouting** | The scouting pool ranked against your tactic, with *Impact* (match-engine fit), *vs CA* (Impact above or below what his CA usually brings: the bargains) and *Avail.* columns, all sortable |
| **World** | The world's best players (by ability) or the next generation (best young potentials) from the whole database, filterable by position / age / nation |
| **Match Lab** | What FM27's match engine rewards, measured: your squad checked against it (fitness, positions, keeper kicking, who is worth more or less than his CA), the levers, attribute values, the familiarity curve, the tactic league and the in-match tweaks |

## How the scoring works (FM27)

**Roles are FM27's own.** Every position has an *in-possession* (IP) role and an
*out-of-possession* (OOP) role, as on the game's tactics screen, so each slot on the pitch
takes two roles. The role lists and the **Key** / **Preferable** attributes of every one
of the 79 roles (40 IP + 39 OOP) were read from the game's own player screens
(Overview, the role list under the pitch, which highlights a role's attributes) - they are
not guesses. Click a slot and the editor shows the key attributes of the roles you picked;
they match what the game shows. They live in `ROLE_DATA` at the top of `engine.js`; if a
game update changes a role, that is the place to fix it.

**Role fit** is the average of the role's *Key* attributes (on the game's 1-20 scale, using
the exact value - 13.8, not "14"). That was checked against the game's own star ratings
for the same player: for 20 role lists (6 players, every role at every position they can
play, in and out of possession) the key-attribute average put the roles in the game's
order 92-95% of the time and picked a top-rated role every time, while every attribute
beyond the key ones - preferable ones included - made the match worse. So preferable
attributes only count as a small tie-breaker (5%), and nothing else is mixed in.

**Match-engine fit** is what the match itself rewards, from
[Fm-Match-Lab](https://github.com/caiograco289/Fm-Match-Lab): six studies that replayed real FM27
matches on the game's own engine (27.0.1) about 190,000 times, changing one thing at a time. Each
attribute moved by 5 points for a whole squad was worth, in expected-goal difference per game
per point: Pace 0.10 (+0.03 in finishing), Acceleration 0.09, Jumping Reach 0.07, Stamina 0.06,
Anticipation 0.05, Dribbling 0.05, Concentration 0.04, Finishing 0.04 (it converts chances rather
than creating them), Balance 0.03, Composure 0.03, Work Rate 0.02 (counted up to 12: it showed
nothing around 12 when squads were levelled, and another tester's season runs found it useless above
12) and Vision 0.02. Everything else -
Passing, Technique, First Touch, Crossing, Tackling, Marking, Positioning, Decisions, Teamwork, Flair... -
showed no clear effect. The match-engine fit is his attributes weighted by those values (1-20 scale,
`ENGINE_WEIGHTS` in `engine.js`), shown as **Impact** (0-100) in Scouting and World and broken down on the
Player Search page. The engine plays on attributes, not on CA, so a high Impact with a modest CA or
value is a bargain. Scouting's **vs CA** column measures exactly that: a straight line of Impact
against CA is fitted over the loaded pool, and each player shows how far he sits above (+) or below
it. Match Lab does the same for your own squad (worth more than their CA / flattered by it).

A slot's **rating** is then:

> (**ratings blend** of role fit and match-engine fit − **position cost**) x footedness x
> **availability** (x potential, if "Factor in potential" is ticked)

- **Ratings** (toolbar): *Blend* (default) = half role fit (50% in-possession + 50% out-of-possession),
  half match-engine fit; *Role fit* = the game's star ratings alone (the old behaviour); *Match engine*
  = the studies alone. The studies changed whole squads and aren't split by position (Dribbling on a
  centre-back isn't worth what it is on a winger), which is why the default keeps the role fit too.
  Goalkeepers always use the role fit.
- **Position cost** uses the game's own 1-20 position ratings (Natural 20, Accomplished 15-19,
  Competent 10-14, Unconvincing 5-9, Awkward 2-4, Ineffectual 1) and takes attribute points off the
  fit: 0 at 20, 0.5 at 15, 2.0 at 10, 4.5 at 5, 7.2 at 1. Calibrated on the studies: capping a team's
  starters at 15 in their positions cost 0.26 points per game, at 10 cost 0.85.
- *Footedness*: wide roles that overlap or cross want the matching foot, inside roles
  (Inside Forward, Inside Winger, Wide Forward) the other one; worth at most about 7%.
- **Availability** (toolbar: Off / Low ±3% / **Normal ±5%** / High ±10%): Injury Proneness and Natural
  Fitness, which decide how often a player is fit and sharp. In the studies match sharpness at 50% cost
  a full point per game and condition at 80% cost 0.8, while consistency, important matches,
  determination, buy-in, current ability, reputation and most personality fields made no measurable
  difference in a match (Natural Fitness isn't even read during one). So availability is the only
  hidden factor left in the rating. The player profile still shows the rest, plus his **tactical
  ability** by the game's own formula (median of Anticipation, Decisions, Composure, Concentration
  and Versatility, minus 1, × 100/19).
- The **Best XI** is a true best-value assignment (Hungarian algorithm), so the first slot
  can no longer take a player who is worth more somewhere else.

**Team Info** has a *What the match engine rewards* panel built on the same studies: fitness today
(tired or rusty starters with their cost, and who needs minutes), starters playing out of position
with their estimated cost in league points, the XI's averages and weakest links on the attributes the
engine values most, and a note when the keeper's Kicking is high (the one clearly negative
attribute). The **Tactic Advisor** adds a rough points-per-game gap to your tactic and what the
studies say about switching: familiarity is an S-curve (under about 20 costs a third of a point a
game, from about 50 a team is at the plateau), it fades between sessions, and since a tactic's
overall strength predicted its results against every style, changing tactic per opponent buys little.

The **Match Lab** tab collects the evidence (Fm-Match-Lab Studies 1-8 and the other FM27 tests they
compare with): your squad checked against it, the levers with their confirmed sizes (sharpness,
condition, positions, familiarity, in-match management, pressing trap, cohesion, mentality, presets,
tweaks), the attribute values, the familiarity curve, the 18-tactic league (every community tactic
above every preset; Gegenpress and Fluid Counter-Attack the best presets, Catenaccio and Park the
Bus the worst) and the in-match Tactical Tweaks (none helps on average; Lock It Down from kick-off
costs about 0.2). Most results come from one club (a promoted Premier League side), the league and
tweak tests from equal synthetic squads, so the sizes are a guide.

The "Hide unfamiliar-position players" box removes anyone rated below Competent (under 10)
for the slot. **Auto-pick roles** sets every slot to the in- and out-of-possession roles
that suit your squad's best players for it.

## Extending it

- Roles and their attributes: `ROLE_DATA` / `ROLE_LISTS` at the top of `engine.js`.
- Rating mix and penalties: `PREF_WEIGHT`, `ENGINE_WEIGHTS` / `ENGINE_PPG` (the Fm-Match-Lab values),
  `RATING_MODELS`, `positionCost20`, `HIDDEN_STRENGTH` and `hiddenProfile` in `engine.js`; formation
  presets: `PRESET_FORMATIONS`.
- Everything on screen: `app.js` / `index.html` / `styles.css`. `roles-data.js` is a leftover
  from the first version and is no longer used.

## CSV import (without the app)

Opening `index.html` directly still works with FM CSV exports (a squad view with the
attribute columns). Players imported that way have no real 1-20 position ratings (position
text is used instead), and no World tab; everything else works the same.

## Files

| File | What it does |
|---|---|
| `server.py` | The local app: serves the page and a small JSON API over the game's memory |
| `fm26mem/` | The read-only memory reader (`layout.py` has every offset) |
| `index.html` | Page structure |
| `styles.css` | All visual styling |
| `engine.js` | Roles, scoring (role fit, match-engine fit), position cost, availability, XI assignment, bench, formation comparison (no DOM) |
| `app.js` | Wires the engine to the page - pitch, tabs, rendering |
| `memory-bridge.js` | Talks to the app's API and converts players for the engine |
| `build_exe.bat` | Builds `dist\FM27Plus.exe` |
| `tests/` | A synthetic FM process (`fakefm.py`) so the reader and the app run without the game: `python tests/test_reader.py`, `python tests/run_fake_server.py` |
| `CALIBRATION.md` | What still has to be checked against the live game (injuries, home-grown status, interest) |
